
Ad-hoc polymorphism: Same function name, different functionality for different types. (compute area in shape)

var max = fun(a:int,b:int) { ret if a<b {a} else {b} }
       ++ fun(a:bool,b:bool) { ret if a {b}else{a} }
       ++ fun(a:Shape,b:Shape) { ret if a.area() < b.area() {a}else{b} }

Parametric polymorphism: Same function name, same functionality for different types.

"A type may be parametrized on types in the same way that a function may be
parametrized on values" (quote from "Lightweight parametric polymorphism for
Oberon")

-------
Type constraint vs type classes

Type constraints: Constraints what is allowed (if not constrained, anything allowed)

c++:
  auto add(auto a, auto b) { return a + b; }

Pyrope:
  let add = fun(a,b) { a + b }           // no constrains
  let add = fun<T>(a:T,b:T) { a + b }    // constrain both to have same type

Type classes: Specifies the only allowed (Must specify what is allowed). Give consent, not constraint.

swift:
  func add<T:Numeric>(a:T, b:T) -> T { a + b }
  func add<T>(a:T, b:T) -> T { a + b }  // compile error


// Feedback from: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E-2y1qHQvTg&t=477s
// https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qh7QdG5RK9E

// swift

protocol Shape {
  func name()      -> String
  func area()      -> Float
  func perimeter() -> Float
}

class Rectangle : Shape {  }
class Circle    : Shape {  }

func print_share_info<T:Shape>(_ s:T) {

}

// Pyrope

let Shape = (
  ,name:fun(self)->(:string)    = _
  ,area:fun(self)->(:Float)     = _
  ,perimeter:fun(self)->(:Float)= _
)

let Rectangle:(...Shape,...OtherAPI) = ( ...)
let Circle = ( ...)          // no need to add :Shape, only if wanted to check that the shape API is implemented

let print_share_info(s:Shape) { }

